PS: Legal/Policy Document Simplifier with Clause-Level Risk Flagging:
Upload terms of service or rental agreements; the tool doesn't just summarize but flags specific clauses as "unusual" or "risky" by comparing against a reference set of standard clauses (RAG-based).

System Architecture: Legal Document Risk Analyzer
1. Core Design Principle

Don't try to build "one LLM call that summarizes." You need a pipeline: parse → segment → classify → compare → score → explain. Each stage should be independently testable and swappable. This is a RAG + classification hybrid, not a pure RAG chatbot.

2. Pipeline Stages
Stage 1: Ingestion & Parsing
Accept PDF, DOCX, plain text, HTML (for web ToS).
Use pdfplumber/unstructured.io for PDF, mammoth or python-docx for DOCX.
Preserve structural metadata: section headers, numbering, page numbers. You'll need this later for citing exactly where a risky clause lives.
Output: clean text + structure tree (sections → subsections → clauses).
Stage 2: Clause Segmentation (this is the hard part, get it right)

Naive token-based chunking (e.g., 512-token sliding window) is wrong for legal docs. A clause can be one sentence or three paragraphs, and splitting mid-clause destroys meaning.

Do semantic clause segmentation:

First pass: rule-based split on numbered/lettered clauses (\d+\.\d+, Section X, Article X) and legal boilerplate markers ("WHEREAS", "NOW THEREFORE").
Second pass: for unstructured docs (raw ToS pages with no numbering), use an LLM call with structured output to segment into logical clauses. Cheap model here (Haiku-class), not your main reasoning model.
Each clause becomes a record: {clause_id, text, section_path, char_offset, doc_id}.

This segmentation quality directly determines your final product quality. Budget real engineering time here, not just prompt engineering.

Stage 3: Reference Corpus (the RAG backbone)

This is what makes "unusual" mean something instead of being a hallucinated LLM opinion.

Build a labeled reference clause database:

Source standard/fair clauses from: EFF's ToS;DR dataset, public rental agreement templates from state bar associations/HUD, Creative Commons legal templates, LawInsider's public clause library (check licensing), your own manually reviewed set.
Each reference entry: {clause_text, category (e.g., "arbitration", "auto-renewal", "liability limitation", "data sharing"), risk_label (standard/cautionary/risky), jurisdiction, source, plain_explanation}.
Minimum viable corpus: 500-1000 labeled clauses across 15-20 categories to start. This will feel small, it's enough for MVP because you're doing similarity + LLM reasoning, not pure retrieval matching.
Store embeddings in a vector DB alongside metadata for filtering by category/jurisdiction.

Categorize clause types up front (don't skip this, it's your taxonomy backbone):

Arbitration/dispute resolution
Auto-renewal/subscription traps
Unilateral modification rights
Liability limitation/indemnification
Data collection/sharing/sale
Termination conditions
Fee structures/penalties
Security deposit terms (rental-specific)
Right of entry (rental-specific)
Governing law/venue
Stage 4: Retrieval + Classification (hybrid, not pure RAG)

For each extracted clause:

Embed the clause (use a model fine-tuned or strong on legal/semantic similarity — text-embedding-3-large or a legal-domain sentence-transformer like nlpaueb/legal-bert derivatives for embeddings if you want domain specificity).
Retrieve top-k (5-8) similar reference clauses from the vector DB, filtered by category if you've pre-classified the clause type.
Rule-based flag layer runs in parallel — regex/keyword detectors for known red-flag patterns (e.g., "sole discretion," "without notice," "binding arbitration," "waive right to jury trial," "automatically renew"). This catches things embedding similarity might miss and gives you deterministic, auditable flags.
LLM reasoning call with structured output: pass the clause + retrieved reference clauses + rule-flags as context. Ask the model to:
Classify clause category
Compare against retrieved standard clauses
Assign risk level (standard / cautionary / risky) with reasoning
Generate plain-language rewrite
Cite which retrieved reference it's deviating from and how

Force structured JSON output (tool use / function calling), never free text here. This is non-negotiable for a product that needs to render risk badges reliably.

json
{
  "clause_id": "c_042",
  "category": "auto_renewal",
  "risk_level": "risky",
  "confidence": 0.87,
