Stage 5: Aggregation & Scoring
Roll up clause-level risk into a document-level risk score (weighted by category severity — arbitration/data-sharing weighted higher than formatting boilerplate).
Generate an executive summary: top 5 riskiest clauses, categories present, overall risk tier.
3. Why Hybrid (RAG + Rules + LLM) Instead of Pure LLM

Pure "ask GPT if this is risky" fails for three reasons:

No grounding — model opinion drifts, inconsistent between runs, no way to explain "risky compared to what."
No auditability — you can't show a user or regulator why something was flagged.
Hallucination risk — LLM may invent legal claims. The rule-layer + retrieval grounding gives you a defensible, explainable flag even before the LLM reasons about it.

The reference corpus is your product's actual IP. The LLM is just the reasoning glue.

4. Tech Stack (concrete)

Given your existing comfort with Next.js/Fastify/Postgres:

Frontend: Next.js. Render document with inline highlight overlays (color-coded by risk), click-to-expand clause explanation panel.
Backend API: Fastify, same as your other stack.
Vector DB: pgvector on Postgres (avoid adding a separate vector DB dependency if you're already on Postgres) or Qdrant if you want dedicated ANN performance at scale.
Document parsing service: separate worker (Python, since unstructured/pdfplumber ecosystem is stronger there) queued via BullMQ/Redis, since parsing + LLM calls are slow and shouldn't block the API.
LLM: Claude for the classification/reasoning step (structured tool-use output). Use a cheaper model for clause segmentation, stronger model for risk reasoning.
Embeddings: OpenAI text-embedding-3-large or Voyage AI's legal/domain embeddings (Voyage has stronger legal-doc performance in benchmarks).
Job queue: Redis + BullMQ, since a full document (50+ clauses) means 50+ LLM calls, needs async processing with progress updates to frontend (SSE/websocket).
5. Critical Non-Negotiables
Disclaimer layer: every output states "not legal advice," confidence scores shown per clause, not just presented as fact.
Confidence thresholding: if retrieval similarity is low (no good reference match) and rule-layer doesn't flag it, mark as "unable to assess" rather than forcing a risk label. Silence is safer than false confidence.
Audit trail: store which reference clauses were retrieved and compared for every flag, so output is explainable, not a black box.
Versioned reference corpus: clause standards evolve (e.g., FTC rules on auto-renewal changed). Version your corpus, don't hardcode it as static.
Eval set: build 50-100 documents with human-labeled ground truth risky clauses. Track precision/recall on risk flags before shipping. This is what separates a real product from a demo.

