1. Sourcing (where the 200 clauses come from)

Don't write these from imagination — pull real clauses from real public documents, then label them yourself. Sources that are free and legally fine to use:

Source	What you get
ToS;DR (Terms of Service Didn't Read)	Community-labeled clauses already tagged good/bad for major platforms — huge head start, some can be directly reused with attribution
Google, Reddit, Spotify, GitHub, Discord ToS (public)	Real "standard" clauses — big companies' legal teams write fairly balanced ToS, good baseline for "standard" label
Sketchy/aggressive apps' ToS (smaller SaaS, data-broker apps)	Good source of "risky" examples — auto-renewal traps, broad data-sharing, forced arbitration
HUD.gov / state bar association rental lease templates	Standard rental clauses (security deposit, entry notice, etc.)
LawInsider.com public clause library	Searchable by clause type, free tier lets you browse real contract language
FTC.gov enforcement actions / blog	Literally lists real risky clauses regulators flagged — great ground truth for "risky" label with citation

You don't write fake legal text. You collect real clauses and label them.

2. The 8 categories (lock these first)
Auto-renewal / subscription terms
Arbitration / dispute resolution / jury waiver
Unilateral modification rights ("we may change these terms at any time")
Liability limitation / indemnification
Data collection / sharing / sale
Termination conditions
Fee structures / hidden penalties
Governing law / venue
3. Labeling process (manual, ~2-3 hrs of focused work)

For each clause you collect, fill this row:

text: <the actual clause, verbatim>
category: <one of the 8>
risk_label: standard | cautionary | risky
explanation: <1-2 sentences on WHY this label>
source: <e.g. "Spotify ToS 2024" or "FTC enforcement action re: Adobe">

Rule of thumb for labeling:

standard — gives user reasonable notice, mutual terms, industry-normal (e.g. 30-day auto-renewal notice)
cautionary — legal but user-unfriendly, worth flagging (e.g. 14-day notice, binding arbitration but with opt-out window)
risky — heavily one-sided, minimal notice, waives significant rights (e.g. "sole discretion," 3-day cancellation window, no opt-out arbitration)

Aim for roughly 25 clauses per category (8 × 25 = 200), a rough mix of ~40% standard / 35% cautionary / 25% risky so your reference set isn't just a "risky clause" list — you need real "this is fine" examples too, or every match will look bad by comparison.

4. Practical collection method

Fastest way to hit 200 without dying of manual copy-paste:

Pick 15-20 real documents (mix of big-company ToS + smaller/sketchier ones + rental templates).
Run each through your Step 2 clause segmentation (the thing you're already building) to auto-split into candidate clauses.
You manually review the segmented output and just assign category + risk_label + explanation to each — you're not writing clauses, just labeling ones already extracted. This also doubles as testing your segmentation logic on real documents.
Store as CSV first, then bulk-load into Postgres once ready.